00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.080
So let's talk about
a simple Ruby app.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.710
We're going to
use these things--

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:54.820
App Engine, Cloud SQL, Cloud
Memorystore, and VPC Connector.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:07:00.480
So let me tell you about what's
changed in the Google Cloud

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.120
world on why this is relevant.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.760
The janky old App Engine
of a couple of years

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:08.880
ago, it was this
all-in-one product.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.590
It came with Datastore.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.570
It came with a special
flavor of memcache.

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.610
It was memcacheg, for Google.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.680
It was different from
regular memcache.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.540
And those services
worked really well.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.260
They worked perfectly
out of the box.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.990
But if you wanted to use
something else, tough luck.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:22.532
You couldn't.

00:07:22.532 --> 00:07:24.750
You couldn't even get
direct network access.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:28.500
You had to use a special
API called URL Fetch.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:30.000
You wanted a different library?

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.103
You couldn't use it.

00:07:31.103 --> 00:07:33.270
You could only get the
libraries that were on there.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.595
And the language runtimes
themselves weren't even

00:07:35.595 --> 00:07:36.470
the open source ones.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.580
They were these special
forks of the languages

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.410
that Google maintained.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:43.530
So in other words, it
was super proprietary.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.720
But last year, we
released something

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:49.440
called second generation
runtimes, which is

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.420
what people expect these days.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.510
It's a fully open
source language runtime.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.170
Has direct network
access, obviously.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.180
And so that was a
big step forward.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:04.650
But the Ruby second
generation runtimes

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.390
run on an entirely
different cluster

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:07.753
from the rest of Google Cloud.

00:08:07.753 --> 00:08:10.170
And they weren't able to talk
to a lot of the Google Cloud

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.220
services.

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:14.310
So also yesterday,
we released something

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.320
called VPC connectors.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:22.110
And this completes the picture
for using App Engine second gen

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:23.220
runtimes and Ruby.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.270
They let you connect to
any VM or any service built

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:31.050
on VMs, which is like
Cloud SQL or Redis, that's

00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.280
running in Google Cloud.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.429
So now you can use all
the parts of Google Cloud

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.309
along with your App Engine app.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.929
And that's another big
takeaway that you should get,

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:43.500
is that Google Cloud
has all these fun toys--

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:48.750
AI, Artificial Intelligence
APIs, Redis, memcache, Spanner,

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:49.903
all these cool things.

00:08:49.903 --> 00:08:51.570
And now with App
Engine you can actually

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.320
use them, which is great.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:53.850
All right.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:55.900
So it's pretty easy.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:57.750
This is the bare bones setup.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:01.200
In your gemfile
or wherever you're

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.600
specifying your Ruby gem, you
want to do 2.5.3 or greater.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.760
It's currently
running Ruby 2.5.3,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.490
and it's going to
be 2.5.5 shortly.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:12.630
Maybe we'll go to 2.6.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:13.800
We'll see.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:16.200
App Engine has something
called an app.yaml.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:18.060
That's where you
configure it and tell it

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:19.290
what it is you're doing.

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:22.240
You need to tell it you're
using the Ruby runtime.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:23.490
You tell it an instance class.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:27.210
There's F1, F2, and F4.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:31.680
The F1 is 128 megabytes of RAM.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.860
And it turns out that it's
not enough RAM to run even

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.270
most basic Ruby apps.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.920
So you need to do an F2.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.130
But I've got really good news.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.818
And you guys are going to be
some of the first to hear it.

00:09:43.818 --> 00:09:45.360
Later this quarter,
we're going to be

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.820
doubling the memory on all
of the second gen instances.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.750
So you'll be able to use an F1
for the same price as the F2.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:55.870
That F1 will be 256 megs, which
is enough for a basic Ruby app.

00:09:55.870 --> 00:09:58.420
And finally, you set something
called an entry point.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:01.530
And this is just the
command that gets run.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.150
It could be a Rails
server command.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:07.810
Or it can be a Puma command
directly, or whatever you want.

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:11.310
And then on the CLI, you just
type in gcloud app deploy.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.940
That's assuming you've
got the gcloud client.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:16.620
If you don't, just install it.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.750
And boom, it should work.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:19.950
All right.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.350
So that would get you just
like a Hello World app.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.900
But Hello World doesn't
really get you very far.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:29.160
So you'll probably-- almost all
apps-- we talked about Rails.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:29.790
Rails is great.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:32.640
Rails usually wants a
relational database.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.310
And it really likes Postgres.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:36.450
That's my favorite.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:41.790
So to set this up, just
click into the Cloud SQL

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.437
part of the Google console.

00:10:43.437 --> 00:10:45.270
And you're going to
fill out this form here.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:46.540
You give it some name.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.390
Generate a password.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:51.080
I have all those-- whoa.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:51.635
Oh, yeah, OK.

00:10:51.635 --> 00:10:52.760
I didn't step through this.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:53.390
OK.

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:54.850
So yeah, you'll give it a--

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:58.220
you just click that Generate
Password button and copy that.

00:10:58.220 --> 00:10:59.630
Pick a region.

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:03.980
You can choose to run it in
the US, west, central, or east.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:04.562
Europe.

00:11:04.562 --> 00:11:06.020
We've got lots and
lots of regions.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:09.050
It's one of the really cool
things about Google Cloud.

00:11:09.050 --> 00:11:14.270
And really important-- and this
is actually a little caveat I

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.370
ran into--

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:16.640
enable a public IP.

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:19.940
Otherwise, it
doesn't seem to work.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.340
In theory, you should be
able to have a private IP

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:24.620
and connect via the VPC Connect.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:28.620
But once we get that working,
we'll document that properly.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:29.920
This is still alpha.

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.990
OK, then, one of
the cool things you

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.420
can do in that app.yaml--
this is an App Engine-specific

00:11:35.420 --> 00:11:37.620
thing-- is you can set
environment variables.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:40.010
So just set your-- that
password you generated,

00:11:40.010 --> 00:11:41.210
just stick it in there.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:42.740
Tell it what your user is.

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:44.810
And tell it what the host is.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.160
That host, after this
screen, after you actually

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:52.970
click the Create, it'll
give you this host.

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:56.270
You just put out
the cloudsql dash.

00:11:56.270 --> 00:12:00.800
There's this special unix socket
that gets created on App Engine

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:03.375
to connect to your
Cloud SQL instances.

00:12:03.375 --> 00:12:05.000
And so that's actually
what it's doing.

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.330
And then in your Rails app
and in your database.yml,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.700
you just reference those
environment variables

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.200
for setting up your database.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:20.080
All right, now.

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:23.260
What I talked about before,
creating a VPC connector.

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:26.710
So by default, when you create
a project in Google Cloud,

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:30.340
you get a virtual private cloud
that has all the [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:33.850
stick VMs that are shielded
from the big, bad, dangerous

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.500
internet.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:36.560
So it's real easy.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:38.690
You just click into here.

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:41.110
You're going to have
to enable VPC access.

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:44.260
For some reason I don't get,
most of the products on Google

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:47.050
Cloud don't work by default. You
have to first enable the API.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:48.070
Then you can use them.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:49.112
I don't know why that is.

00:12:49.112 --> 00:12:50.810
I'm sure there's a
good reason for it.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:53.470
But it makes for awkwardness
in getting started.

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:56.830
OK, then you're going to click
into a serverless VPC access

00:12:56.830 --> 00:12:58.510
and just click
Create a Connector.

00:12:58.510 --> 00:12:59.080
Boom.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:00.250
You tell it which network.

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:01.998
You get this default
one by default.

00:13:01.998 --> 00:13:03.790
You're going to need
to type in an IP range

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:06.280
with a dash 28 CIDR range.

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:10.460
Just use the default one that's
in that little help text there.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:13.130
And there you go.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.180
Now you're going to
create a Redis instance.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.450
So again, you just
click onto Memorystore.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:18.860
Create it.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.240
Give it a name.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:20.952
Pick the region.

00:13:20.952 --> 00:13:22.910
You probably want the
region to be the same one

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.010
that you picked for
App Engine, because you

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:27.320
want it to be in nice,
fast internet access.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.755
And that is basically
all you want.

00:13:29.755 --> 00:13:31.880
You could choose, there's
basic and standard tiers.

00:13:31.880 --> 00:13:33.255
The standard tiers
have failover.

00:13:33.255 --> 00:13:35.150
But we don't need
that for our demo.

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:37.550
And then we're going to
configure the app to use Redis.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:38.050
That's all.

00:13:38.050 --> 00:13:44.090
You stick that in your
production config.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:45.470
Tell it use that.

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.750
And you see we're
using that Redis

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:53.890
URL, which I had back here in--

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.280
so back in that
app.yaml, we also

00:13:55.280 --> 00:14:00.220
see that VPC access connector.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.780
You have to put that in
to make the VPC work.

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:06.180
And then you stick in that Redis
URL at the very bottom there.

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:09.200
And that's the address
of the Redis instance

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:11.250
you just created.

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:14.330
And then it will all work.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:14.850
All right.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.170
So I'm not going to actually
go through all of that live,

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:21.950
because it takes 15 minutes.

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:27.260
But we can just pull
up a working app,

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:28.270
prove that it's working.

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:28.770
OK.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.620
Can I get the--

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:33.410
there we go.

00:14:33.410 --> 00:14:34.570
Cool.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:36.850
So I created this app.

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:39.480
I'll pull it up here.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.405
Let's make this
not terribly small.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.330
So here's this app.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:58.630
It's just a simple
app with two models.

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:07.840
And let's-- so here's that
app.yaml that I was showing you

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:08.340
before.

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:09.930
This is all the live stuff.

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:11.605
I guess it has my
credentials in it.

00:15:11.605 --> 00:15:13.230
I'll have to destroy
this database now.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:17.800
Otherwise, heaven forbid,
you could put data in there.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:19.380
So that's the basic app.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.110
And boom, it is
running right here.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:27.300
You can see you get this
little appspot.com domain.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:28.220
This is an app.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:33.570
It tracks watches because I've
been collecting watches lately.

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:37.560
And it's a fun thing
to put in a database.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:41.250
And you can put in
a URL, and it works.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:44.670
And that is a Rails app
running on App Engine.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:49.650
For some reason, it's
been super slow in the--

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.570
you can see it's
loading up right now.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:52.860
I have no idea why that is.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.550
But again, it's
alpha, so it's slow.

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:58.650
And man, that is really painful.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:15:59.280
Really painful.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:15:59.610
All right.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.350
Well, it's not supposed
to work that way.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:04.870
But the demo gods are not
looking favorably on us.

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:05.370
All right.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:10.380
So let's switch back
into the presentation.

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:11.840
OK.

00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:15.230
Again, put some
questions in Dory.

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:15.730
All right.

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:17.560
Now let's get to the fun part.

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:22.420
So let's look at a
world-spanning Ruby app.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:25.660
Now, I think when, at
least for me, when I first

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:27.880
got introduced to
cloud computing,

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:29.240
this is what I wanted.

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:31.300
I wanted a cloud that
spanned the world.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:35.710
And I've talked to lots
of customers of Heroku

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.470
and Google Cloud and Docker.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:45.070
And there's, I'd say 1 out
of 5, maybe 1 out of 2 times,

00:16:45.070 --> 00:16:46.795
they were like, they
say, well, can you

00:16:46.795 --> 00:16:47.920
run it in different places?

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:48.590
Yes.

00:16:48.590 --> 00:16:50.440
Can I run it in all
those places at once?

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.270
No.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:54.040
It's something that's
really hard to do.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:56.355
But it's something
that people want.

00:16:56.355 --> 00:16:56.980
It's the cloud.

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:58.120
It's not located anywhere.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:00.040
It's just kind of ephemeral.

00:17:00.040 --> 00:17:02.590
So you want a cloud
that covers the world.

00:17:02.590 --> 00:17:04.599
But really, you end
up getting this.

00:17:04.599 --> 00:17:05.710
It's like, yeah.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.085
You can put it
wherever you want.

00:17:07.085 --> 00:17:09.040
Central, east, west--
we've got all of them.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.980
Pick one.

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:12.560
So, yeah, it's great.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:14.290
It's still super
awesome to not have

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.819
to manage all these
things, not have

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:17.230
to go down to the data center.

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:19.690
But it's not that
cloud of our dreams.

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:22.550
But it's actually
possible to build.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.369
So let's build it.

00:17:24.369 --> 00:17:27.010
So this is what we're
going to use for this.

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:31.600
Going to use the new Cloud
Run on GKE product that got

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:32.410
launched yesterday.

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:34.820
It is super cool.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:39.040
If you are running Kubernetes
in your organization

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:41.170
but want to have a
serverless experience,

00:17:41.170 --> 00:17:43.930
it is a smoking hot product.

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:45.440
You should probably
have, there's

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:48.190
probably someone in your
company that's the Kubernetes

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:51.550
operator, maybe two people.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:53.980
But then you put Cloud
Run on top of it,

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:57.980
and you don't need to worry
about the Kubernetes stuff.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.090
We'll also use Spanner.

00:17:59.090 --> 00:18:01.520
So one of the hardest
parts about creating

00:18:01.520 --> 00:18:05.510
an app in multiple
locations is the data.

00:18:05.510 --> 00:18:08.390
It's really easy to just
run something that returns

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:11.485
Hello World, and that's it.

00:18:11.485 --> 00:18:13.610
You can run that in multiple
places, but who cares?

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:16.070
Almost all apps need
data, and data is heavy.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.910
And so it doesn't like
to move around a lot.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:20.285
But we've got this really cool
technology called Spanner.

00:18:20.285 --> 00:18:21.490
And we'll back it with that.

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.620
You can use Cloud Memory,
Redis, the same way

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.250
that we did in the other app.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.280
And again, that VPC connector.

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:30.330
And then to wrap
it all together,

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:34.182
we'll use Cloudflare
to do the georouting.

00:18:34.182 --> 00:18:36.390
Well, there was a really
cool map of the world there.

00:18:36.390 --> 00:18:38.200
But that's not loading
for some reason.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:41.538
So imagine there's a map here.

00:18:41.538 --> 00:18:43.080
We're going to run
this in US Central

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:46.440
1, which is Iowa,
in EU West, which

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:49.240
is in Belgium, and in Asia.

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:52.210
It's going to be one app, three
continents, load balanced.

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:55.530
So it'll go to whichever
continent you are closest to.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:57.810
And it'll have
automatic failover.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.250
Here's the architecture.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:03.690
So on the front, like I said,
the hard parts about this

00:19:03.690 --> 00:19:05.560
are the load balancing
and the data.

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:07.660
Doing the app is
actually pretty easy.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.620
So we'll use Cloudflare
for a geoload balancer.

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:13.920
Currently, the Google
Cloud load balancer

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.650
does not work well
with Cloud Run.

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:19.870
Hopefully, by the
end of the year,

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.630
that will not be the case.

00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:24.190
So you could just keep it
all in the Google Cloud.

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:25.750
But frankly, it doesn't matter.

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:27.660
Cloudflare is a great product.

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:28.160
It worked.

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:30.820
It was super-- it was actually
one of the easier parts

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:32.450
of this whole demo to set up.

00:19:32.450 --> 00:19:34.330
I had never done--

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:36.340
I'd never used this part
of Cloudflare before.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:39.370
But it took me under
an hour from learning

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:41.140
on how to set it up.

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:44.200
It'll geobalance
to the code running

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.068
in any one of these
three regions.

00:19:46.068 --> 00:19:48.610
When I made this slide, I was
going to try to use App Engine.

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:52.340
But it turns out Cloud Run
worked a little bit better.

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:54.730
The reason for that,
by the way, is you

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:59.230
can only have one App Engine
app per Google Cloud project.

00:19:59.230 --> 00:20:03.340
So that makes it hard to have
an app running in three places.

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:04.805
You can create
multiple projects.

00:20:04.805 --> 00:20:07.180
But then those apps can't all
connect to the same Spanner

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:10.460
instance, because the apps
connect to the Spanner instance

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:11.800
in their project.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:15.930
Projects are these fenced
bounds in Google Cloud.

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:20.670
Then I'll have a cache layer,
and then the Spanner database

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:21.920
and back.

00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:24.780
So here's some caveats.

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:27.550
There is no Active Record
support for Spanner.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:29.308
If you google it,
you will find a gem.

00:20:29.308 --> 00:20:30.850
It hasn't had an
update in two years.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.190
So it's not based on
the latest Ruby version.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.110
It's not based on the
latest Rails version.

00:20:35.110 --> 00:20:38.570
It does actually work on the
versions that it talks about.

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.520
But it was a proof of concept.

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:43.060
It was built by somebody
who used to work at Google.

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:46.870
And it did work as
a proof of concept.

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:48.850
The Ruby team at
Google is hoping

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.390
to build an Active Record
adapter for Spanner

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:55.310
later this year.

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:58.000
That means that hopefully
next year, it will exist.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:20:59.340
I would love it.

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:03.640
If that does exist, doing this
will become exceedingly simple,

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.580
because without
Active Record, Rails

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:09.920
isn't very friendly to use.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.690
Caveat number two
is you're going

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:14.260
to need to use the Google
Cloud client library,

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:18.550
and it does not support
process forking.

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:19.810
Maybe you've delved into this.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:24.880
But most modern servers,
they'll fork the process

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:27.730
and get better concurrency.

00:21:27.730 --> 00:21:33.190
That will just break
the GRPC library, which

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:35.230
the cloud client's based on.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:39.970
And so you just need to
set your app to not fork.

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:43.580
If you're using Puma, there's
a simple flag to do that.

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:48.160
And third caveat is, oh
my god, it is expensive.

00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:51.650
This is not for
the faint of heart.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:52.910
It is really cool.

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:55.120
And it's almost kind of
cool how expensive it is.

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.320
How expensive is it?

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:59.090
It's about a Tesla
Model 3 per month.

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:02.830
And that's if you use the
barest-bone configuration.

00:22:02.830 --> 00:22:07.030
So the barest-bone
Spanner configuration

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:12.050
that runs on three continents
is about $27,000 per month.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:13.150
Yeah, it's silly.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:14.830
It's silly expensive.

00:22:14.830 --> 00:22:18.430
The good news is, if you're
a really big company, that's

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:19.280
a Spanner instance.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:21.950
You can have up to 100
databases on an instance.

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:25.240
So each database is actually
going to cost you about $270

00:22:25.240 --> 00:22:27.260
a month, which is
super reasonable.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.730
So if you work for a
big company and they

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:33.010
decide to use Spanner
for a bunch of things,

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:34.810
it starts to become
pretty cost-effective.

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:37.030
Or if you're at
really big scale.

00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:41.200
I mean, if you're running
the next super-hot startup,

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:46.060
$27,000 a month to have global
reach is pretty reasonable.

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:48.880
There's also a
whole GKE cluster.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:50.980
Each cluster in
each continent has

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:56.320
45 gigs of RAM and
three nodes and costs

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.550
another several thousand
dollars a month.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:22:59.990
So it's pretty pricey.

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:01.137
But it's cool.

00:23:01.137 --> 00:23:01.720
You can do it.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:04.820
OK, so how do you do it?

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:07.750
First, you have to
create a Spanner client.

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:10.780
I just created that
little initializer.

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:13.180
And this is straight
out of the Spanner docs.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:16.210
And you can see at the very end
there, the little dollar sign.

00:23:16.210 --> 00:23:18.970
That makes it a global and to
just access this from anywhere

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:22.170
in your Rails app.

00:23:22.170 --> 00:23:24.460
Then you need to populate it.

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:27.240
The cool thing about Spanner
is it's plain vanilla SQL.

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:29.790
So this is just some plain SQL.

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:33.240
I just created it and pasted
it in on the Spanner console

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:35.790
and created my table.

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.430
The app we're going to create
is just a very simple posts

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:39.465
app that has messages.

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:46.035
And like I said before, there's
no Active Record support.

00:23:46.035 --> 00:23:48.160
Fortunately, there's this
thing in Rails now called

00:23:48.160 --> 00:23:50.530
Active Model, which
is a lot of Active

00:23:50.530 --> 00:23:53.097
Record without the database.

00:23:53.097 --> 00:23:55.180
But you just need to
re-create some of the methods

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:58.430
that Active Record would
otherwise give you.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:01.390
So what's going on here?

00:24:01.390 --> 00:24:02.890
When I was more
ambitious, there was

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:04.720
an image uploaded on this app.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:07.210
It didn't quite work.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:13.000
We have the Active Model
in this post class.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:17.740
We also have a few other
things, like these URLs helpers,

00:24:17.740 --> 00:24:20.740
that just make things work.

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.440
Unlike an Active Record app, you
need to create this attribute

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:24.190
accessor.

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:27.090
These are just the
various attributes

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:30.220
that's in the database
that lets you access them.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.380
And you can do validation.

00:24:32.380 --> 00:24:35.200
And that's the basics
of creating a model.

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:36.820
Then we just need
to re-create some

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:40.100
of the methods Active
Record gives you.

00:24:40.100 --> 00:24:41.350
Here's a simple finder.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:42.760
You pass in an ID.

00:24:42.760 --> 00:24:45.790
Then it just executes this
SQL, where you find a post

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:47.170
and return the first one.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:50.300
There should only be one,
but better safe than sorry.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:53.410
So that would be post.find.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:55.950
Then once you have one, if
you want to save it, boom.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.150
You just do a transaction
and insert the stuff

00:24:58.150 --> 00:24:59.200
into the database.

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:03.820
I'm just passing in whatever
got set on that model.

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:08.830
And you could do a Find All,
where you just do a select*

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:12.900
and limit it to--

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:15.400
if you have a lot of stuff, you
don't want too many of them.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:17.020
So this is re-creating
the methods

00:25:17.020 --> 00:25:19.450
that Active Record would
give you by default.

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:21.847
Again, if we make an Active
Record Spanner adapter,

00:25:21.847 --> 00:25:22.930
you won't need to do this.

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:24.110
It'll be really easy.

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:24.760
All right.

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.320
Then setting up Cloudflare.

00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:28.930
So Cloudflare, you
just add a site.

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:30.430
You create these
things called pods.

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:33.070
Pods are nominally
collections of servers.

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.443
But since with
serverless you are

00:25:35.443 --> 00:25:37.360
getting a collection of
servers, you basically

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:44.510
just map each of these to one
of your deployed instances.

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.047
And then you create
a load balancer.

00:25:46.047 --> 00:25:48.130
And the load balancer is
just saying, load balance

00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:49.540
between these pods.

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:52.600
And you can see at the very
top there, you give the URL.

00:25:52.600 --> 00:25:56.620
In order for Cloudflare to work,
you need to be doing your DNS.

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:59.080
And it will automatically
create all the DNS magic.

00:25:59.080 --> 00:26:00.955
You could also create
some health checks here

00:26:00.955 --> 00:26:02.780
to just make sure it's working.

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:03.490
OK.

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:08.800
So let's switch into
demo mode again.

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:12.580
Wake up my computer.

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:21.070
Now, the demo gods were
not friendly to me.

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:25.150
And for some reason,
the-- so here's the app.

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:33.510
And maybe my internet's
not even working.

00:26:33.510 --> 00:26:34.150
Oh, wait.

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:35.080
I see.

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:35.780
Wrong URL.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.540
Boom.

00:26:41.540 --> 00:26:42.040
All right.

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:45.192
So it says this app is
being served from the EU.

00:26:45.192 --> 00:26:46.900
So the demo gods were
not friendly to me,

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:48.317
but it actually
helped demonstrate

00:26:48.317 --> 00:26:49.720
some of the power of this.

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:52.300
For some reason, I could
not get the US version

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:53.350
of this app working.

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.630
Something got borked
in the configuration.

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:57.210
But guess what?

00:26:57.210 --> 00:27:00.430
The Cloudflare georouting
also does failover.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:03.770
So it's routing us to the
EU version of the app.

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:06.130
And that's basically
just pulling out

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:09.010
a config var that lets you know
where it's being served from,

00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:10.640
that little message at the top.

00:27:10.640 --> 00:27:15.380
So what this looks
like in Google Cloud--

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:16.474
whoa, too big.

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:26.290
So you go into Google
Cloud's console,

00:27:26.290 --> 00:27:28.810
and you tap on Cloud Run.

00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:32.730
And basically, you could
just create a service.

00:27:32.730 --> 00:27:34.180
Actually, let's deploy.

00:27:34.180 --> 00:27:36.110
Let's show a full deploy here.

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:39.065
I'm going to go to
my command line.

00:27:39.065 --> 00:27:42.980
And here is the directory.

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:45.400
So here's this Hi World app.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:51.495
I need to switch to the project.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.457
So again, we talked
about those projects.

00:28:00.457 --> 00:28:01.665
This app has its own project.

00:28:04.070 --> 00:28:04.570
All right.

00:28:04.570 --> 00:28:06.590
So now I'm going to
use that project.

00:28:06.590 --> 00:28:08.290
And now I'm going to do a build.

00:28:08.290 --> 00:28:09.800
Let's just see.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:14.210
I'll just pull it out of
my command history here.

00:28:14.210 --> 00:28:14.710
All right.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:29.585
So gcloud builds submit.

00:28:29.585 --> 00:28:30.460
And you give the tag.

00:28:30.460 --> 00:28:33.840
So we have this other
product called Cloud Build.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:41.390
And it takes a Docker file
and builds it for you.

00:28:41.390 --> 00:28:43.720
So I just told it to
build that and tag it

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:46.930
with this-- gcr.io is
our container registry.

00:28:46.930 --> 00:28:48.910
And it's going to
fetch this thing

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:52.040
and pull it all
up into the cloud.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:53.920
You can also do the
build locally with Docker

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:56.550
and then just push it up there.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.680
It actually will work
faster if you build locally,

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:02.620
because right now,
Cloud Build does not

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:05.760
cache your layers in Docker.

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:08.090
It just goes through all
the regular build stuff.

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:09.700
This is just plain
vanilla Docker.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:10.900
Nothing special about this.

00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:13.210
That's the power of Cloud Run.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.150
Once it's built, I
can create a service.

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:18.640
And I just go into Cloud Run.

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:21.080
Here, let's start
from the beginning.

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:27.487
I just go into Cloud Run,
click Create Service,

00:29:27.487 --> 00:29:30.970
pick my container image.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:32.145
There we go.

00:29:32.145 --> 00:29:32.770
Give it a name.

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:42.985
Now, when you pick a region,
this is Cloud Run not on GKE.

00:29:42.985 --> 00:29:44.860
Right now, it's only
available in US central.

00:29:44.860 --> 00:29:48.130
But then these are my GKE
clusters that I set up before.

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:49.660
I just pick which one I want.

00:29:49.660 --> 00:29:51.780
Can deploy it to Asia.

00:29:51.780 --> 00:29:56.530
And we allow it to
connect to the internet.

00:29:56.530 --> 00:29:58.120
Optional settings.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.840
You can pick how much memory
each instance of this app gets.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:04.450
You can add any config
variables you want here.

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:05.950
So on this app,
I've got a region.

00:30:10.423 --> 00:30:11.417
Click Create.

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:17.760
And it's going to deploy.

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:21.260
And that's all it takes to
deploy something to Cloud Run.

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:23.450
And then over here,
just to show you

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:30.440
what's happening in Cloudflare,
we've got this load balancer.

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:31.510
Is that visible?

00:30:35.470 --> 00:30:41.080
So in Cloudflare, there's
this little traffic tab here.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:44.750
And you set up a load balancer.

00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:45.590
I've got this one.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:48.440
It's hosting the demo subdomain.

00:30:48.440 --> 00:30:51.900
And you can see it's
got two pools active.

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:53.990
Here's that US pool
that wasn't working.

00:30:53.990 --> 00:30:57.050
I just turned it off because
it was having problems.

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:59.810
But now, it just routes to my
European pool and my Asia pool.

00:30:59.810 --> 00:31:03.940
If we take a look
at one of those,

00:31:03.940 --> 00:31:06.070
this is just the
default IP address

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:08.260
that I get on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:31:08.260 --> 00:31:10.630
I just stuck that in there
straight out of the console.

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:11.500
I'll show you that.

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:21.298
Here's that European
version of the app.

00:31:21.298 --> 00:31:21.840
There's that.

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:27.490
And I basically just copied
and pasted that into here.

00:31:27.490 --> 00:31:32.370
And here we have the
working Hello World app.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:33.330
Super small.

00:31:33.330 --> 00:31:35.740
Wrong way.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:42.686
Hello Cloud Next 2019.

00:31:42.686 --> 00:31:44.108
Boom.

00:31:44.108 --> 00:31:46.460
And there it is.

00:31:46.460 --> 00:31:47.702
Hello Cloud Next.

00:31:47.702 --> 00:31:49.160
Second time I've
made that message.

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:50.400
OK, it works.

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:50.900
Cool.

00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:54.880
Being served from the EU because
the US is down right now.

00:31:54.880 --> 00:31:57.770
And that's a
world-spanning Ruby app.

00:31:57.770 --> 00:32:01.540
Can we go back to the my slides?

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:02.620
Thank you.

00:32:02.620 --> 00:32:03.820
World-spanning Ruby app.

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.292
You saw it here first.

00:32:05.292 --> 00:32:06.750
There's nothing
special about Ruby.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:10.720
You could do this with,
really, any Docker container--

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:13.290
Node, Bash, whatever you want.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:16.290
Just need to list it on port 80.

00:32:16.290 --> 00:32:16.810
All right.

00:32:16.810 --> 00:32:17.310
Wrapping up.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:22.950
So I think one of the
points of this talk

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:26.100
is Google Cloud has a lot
of cool building blocks.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:27.330
There's Spanner.

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:31.140
There's machine learning APIs.

00:32:31.140 --> 00:32:32.400
There's all these cool things.

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.352
And what's really fun is
in just a couple of days

00:32:35.352 --> 00:32:37.560
or a couple of hours,
depending on how good you are--

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.360
took me a couple of days because
I'm a really bad programmer--

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:41.640
you can snap them
together and create

00:32:41.640 --> 00:32:42.723
something really powerful.

00:32:45.370 --> 00:32:47.910
Second takeaway, Ruby is
on the cloud in a big way.

00:32:47.910 --> 00:32:49.540
It's on all those services.

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:51.420
So if you've been waiting
to use Google Cloud

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:56.340
but were deterred by the lack of
Ruby support, it is there now.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:33:00.110
And again, to try it right
now, there is the URL.

